{
  "id": "80648",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Unit Key Equations and Key Terms",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "odeb7rnr27vwcmf",
                "text": "Key Equations"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3426781368",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Coming Soon"
              }
            ],
            "id": "00zpaawps8l6wt8",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "1hbciirbtpmce1n",
                "text": "Key Terms"
              }
            ],
            "id": "onc2gh2kwpp4o5q",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "acid ionization"
              },
              {
                "text": " A reaction involving the transfer of a proton from an acid to water, yielding hydronium ions and the conjugate base of the acid."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1154617971",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "acid–base indicator"
              },
              {
                "text": " A weak acid or base that changes color when it goes from its acidic to its basic form."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4077421321",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "acidic"
              },
              {
                "text": " Describes a solution in which [H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "] > [OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "]."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2062413126",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "acid-ionization constant ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "a"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "text": " The equilibrium constant for an acid-ionization reaction. Also called "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "acid-dissociation constant"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3190721979",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "acids"
              },
              {
                "text": " Substances that produce H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " when dissolved in water."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2868153318",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "amphiprotic"
              },
              {
                "text": " Describes species that may either donate or accept a proton."
              }
            ],
            "id": "553084773",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "amphoteric"
              },
              {
                "text": " Describes species that can act as either an acid or a base."
              }
            ],
            "id": "902919446",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "analyte"
              },
              {
                "text": " A chemical species of interest in an analysis."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3706528178",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "anion"
              },
              {
                "text": " A negatively charged ion."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4001322093",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "autoionization"
              },
              {
                "text": " The process in which water spontaneously produces low concentrations of hydronium and hydroxide ions through the transfer of protons between water molecules."
              }
            ],
            "id": "391583379",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "base ionization"
              },
              {
                "text": " A reaction involving the transfer of a proton from water to a base, yielding hydroxide ions and the conjugate acid of the base."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2318191564",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "base-ionization constant ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "b"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "text": " The equilibrium constant for a base-ionization reaction. Also called base-dissociation constant."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1108675033",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "bases"
              },
              {
                "text": " Substances that produce OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " when dissolved in water."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2761563126",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "basic"
              },
              {
                "text": " Describes a solution in which [H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "] < [OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "]."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3159776698",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "Brønsted-Lowry acid"
              },
              {
                "text": " A substance that donates a proton."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1504174572",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "Brønsted-Lowry base"
              },
              {
                "text": " A substance that accepts a proton."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1407775767",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "buffer"
              },
              {
                "text": " A mixture of appreciable amounts of a weak acid–base pair; the pH of a buffer resists change when small amounts of acid or base are added."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2249889662",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "cation"
              },
              {
                "text": " A positively charged ion."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3595363824",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "color-change interval"
              },
              {
                "text": " A range in pH over which the color change of an indicator is observed."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2978242277",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "compound"
              },
              {
                "text": " A pure substance that can be broken down into simpler substances by chemical changes."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1375232618",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "conjugate acid"
              },
              {
                "text": " Substance formed when a base gains a proton."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1207441413",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "conjugate acid–base pair"
              },
              {
                "text": " An acid and a base that differ only by the presence or absence of a proton (H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " ion)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "219670553",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "conjugate base"
              },
              {
                "text": " Substance formed when an acid loses a proton."
              }
            ],
            "id": "921025228",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "diprotic acid"
              },
              {
                "text": " An acid that has two ionizable hydrogen atoms per molecule."
              }
            ],
            "id": "904761469",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "diprotic base"
              },
              {
                "text": " A base that will accept two protons."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3792519001",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "equivalence point"
              },
              {
                "text": " The point in a titration at which the added solute completely reacts with the solute in the initial solution. (In an acid–base reaction, the point at which the number of moles of acid is stoichiometrically equal to the number of moles of base.)"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3624582175",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "Henderson-Hasselbalch equation"
              },
              {
                "text": " A logarithmic version of the acid ionization constant expression, conveniently formatted for calculating the pH of buffer solutions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "277125633",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "hydrolysis"
              },
              {
                "text": " A chemical reaction in which a molecule of water breaks one or more chemical bonds."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2501918501",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "ion"
              },
              {
                "text": " An atom that has gained or lost electrons and therefore is electrically charged."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3654401280",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "ionization"
              },
              {
                "text": " The conversion of an atom or molecule into an ion through the gain or loss of one or more electrons."
              }
            ],
            "id": "547778773",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "ion-product constant for water ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "K"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "w"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "text": " The equilibrium constant for the autoionization of water."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1880820755",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "leveling effect"
              },
              {
                "text": " The observation that acid–base strength is limited to that of the solvent’s characteristic acid and base species. In water, all strong acids have the same strength and are completely converted into solutions of H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions, and all strong bases are similarly completely converted into solutions of OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2934868220",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "monoprotic acid"
              },
              {
                "text": " An acid that has one ionizable hydrogen atom per molecule."
              }
            ],
            "id": "735305563",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "monoprotic base"
              },
              {
                "text": " A base that will accept a single proton."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2532743411",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "neutral"
              },
              {
                "text": " Describes a solution in which [H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "] = [OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "]."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3274300602",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "neutralization reaction"
              },
              {
                "text": " A reaction between stoichiometrically equivalent amounts of an acid and a base; a reaction between an acid and a base that produces a salt and water."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1550087553",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "oxyacid"
              },
              {
                "text": " A compound with acidic properties, whose molecules contain a central nonmetallic atom bonded to one or more O atoms, at least one of which is bonded to an ionizable H atom."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2980479962",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "percent ionization"
              },
              {
                "text": " The ratio of the concentration of an ionized species to the initial (nominal) concentration of the species, expressed as a percentage."
              }
            ],
            "id": "93823429",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "pH"
              },
              {
                "text": " The negative logarithm (base 10) of the hydronium ion concentration in aqueous solution."
              }
            ],
            "id": "370665791",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "pOH"
              },
              {
                "text": " The negative logarithm (base 10) of the hydroxide ion concentration in aqueous solution."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2168207414",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "polyprotic acid"
              },
              {
                "text": " An acid that has more than one ionizable hydrogen atom per molecule."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2605097306",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "polyprotic base"
              },
              {
                "text": " A base that will accept more than one proton."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1689356498",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "proton"
              },
              {
                "text": " A positively charged subatomic particle located in the nucleus of the atom."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2422592070",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "solvation shell"
              },
              {
                "text": " The layer of solvent molecules that are closest to, and directly interact with, a dissolved ion or molecule."
              }
            ],
            "id": "394227336",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "spectator ions"
              },
              {
                "text": " Ions that are unchanged by a reaction but present in the reaction mixture. They appear in overall ionic equations but not in net ionic equations."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1212540556",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "stepwise ionization"
              },
              {
                "text": " A process in which a polyprotic acid is ionized by losing protons sequentially."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3395403137",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "strong acid"
              },
              {
                "text": " An acid that completely dissociates or ionizes in water to produce hydronium ion."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2116517134",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "strong base"
              },
              {
                "text": " A base that completely dissociates or ionizes in water to produce hydroxide ion."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3682456166",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "titrant"
              },
              {
                "text": " A solution containing a known concentration of substance that will react with the analyte in a titration analysis."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2848172763",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "titration curve"
              },
              {
                "text": " A plot of some sample property (such as pH) versus volume of added titrant."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3614149029",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "tripotic acid"
              },
              {
                "text": " An acid that has three ionizable hydrogen atoms per molecule."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3364434098",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "weak acid"
              },
              {
                "text": " An acid that only partially dissociates or ionizes in water to produce hydronium ion."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3116960688",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "weak base"
              },
              {
                "text": " A base that only partially dissociates or ionizes in water to produce hydroxide ion."
              }
            ],
            "id": "604628595",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
        "id": "3742474130",
